insert into public.questions (id, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('q4-001',4,'4.1','mcq',1,$q$A new web server is deployed straight from installation media and immediately compromised through an unused sample application. Which practice was skipped?$q$,$q$["Load balancing","Hardening against a secure baseline","Log aggregation","Capacity planning"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Hardening removes default/sample content, disables unneeded services, changes defaults, and patches - applied via a secure baseline BEFORE exposure. Load balancing, logging, and capacity planning would not have removed the vulnerable sample app.$q$),
('q4-002',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Which sequence correctly describes working with secure baselines?$q$,$q$["Deploy, establish, maintain","Establish, deploy, maintain","Maintain, establish, deploy","Deploy, maintain, establish"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Establish the approved configuration first, deploy it to systems, then maintain it - auditing for drift and updating as patches/threats evolve. The exam expects this establish->deploy->maintain order.$q$),
('q4-003',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Wi-Fi signal extends into the public parking lot where an attacker connects comfortably. Which activity would have identified and prevented this?$q$,$q$["Penetration test of the web application","Site survey with heat map to tune AP placement and power","Increasing DHCP scope","Enabling SSID broadcast"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A site survey maps RF propagation; heat maps show spillage beyond walls so AP power/placement can be tuned. Web app pen tests, DHCP sizing, and SSID broadcast do not control radio coverage.$q$),
('q4-004',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Which WPA3 feature specifically defeats offline dictionary attacks against captured wireless handshakes?$q$,$q$["TKIP","Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE)","WEP initialization vectors","MAC filtering"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SAE replaces the WPA2 4-way PSK handshake with a password-authenticated key exchange that cannot be brute-forced offline from a capture. TKIP and WEP are broken legacy mechanisms; MAC filtering is trivially bypassed.$q$),
('q4-005',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A company wants per-employee wireless credentials that can be revoked individually when someone leaves. What should be deployed?$q$,$q$["WPA3-Personal with a strong passphrase","WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X and RADIUS","Open network with captive portal","Shared PSK rotated annually"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Enterprise mode authenticates each user via 802.1X/RADIUS with individual credentials - departures are revoked centrally without touching anyone else. Any shared-passphrase scheme requires re-keying every device whenever one person leaves.$q$),
('q4-006',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Employees use personal phones for corporate email. Security wants corporate data separated and remotely wipeable WITHOUT touching personal photos. Which capability provides this?$q$,$q$["Full device wipe only","MDM containerization separating a managed work profile","Disabling personal app stores","Jailbreak detection alone"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Containerization creates an encrypted, managed work partition - corporate apps/data live there and can be selectively wiped, preserving personal content (the BYOD privacy balance). Full wipes destroy personal data; the other options do not separate anything.$q$),
('q4-007',4,'4.2','mcq',1,$q$A vulnerability scan finds a critical flaw on a server nobody recognizes; it appears in no inventory. Which process failure does this expose?$q$,$q$["Change management","Asset management","Incident response","Awareness training"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Unknown, untracked systems mean asset management (inventory, ownership, tracking) failed - you cannot patch, monitor, or scope what you do not know exists. The other processes depend on inventory being right.$q$),
('q4-008',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Before donating old laptops, the IT team must ensure data cannot be recovered, while keeping the drives usable. Which sanitization method fits?$q$,$q$["Degaussing","Overwriting/secure wipe (or crypto-erase)","Physical shredding","Deleting files and emptying the recycle bin"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Overwriting (or cryptographic erase of a self-encrypting drive) destroys data but leaves hardware functional for reuse/donation. Degaussing destroys drive electronics; shredding destroys the device; file deletion leaves trivially recoverable data.$q$),
('q4-009',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Why does decommissioning require certificate and credential revocation, not just data wiping?$q$,$q$["Certificates slow the wipe process","Valid credentials/certs tied to a dead system can still authenticate or impersonate it elsewhere","Revocation is required for recycling","Wiping automatically revokes certificates"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Service accounts, API keys, and certificates outlive the hardware - an attacker holding them can impersonate the retired system or reuse its access. Revocation closes that door; wiping only addresses data ON the device.$q$),
('q4-010',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$Which vulnerability scan type produces the MOST complete and accurate results on internal systems?$q$,$q$["Non-credentialed external scan","Credentialed scan with authenticated access","Passive traffic analysis only","Port scan with banner grabbing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Credentialed scans log in and inspect from inside: installed packages, missing patches, configurations - far fewer false positives/negatives. Non-credentialed shows the outside-attacker view; passive analysis and banner grabbing see only what traffic/services reveal.$q$),
('q4-011',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A scanner reports a critical vulnerability, but investigation proves the affected component is not installed. What is this result called and why does it matter?$q$,$q$["False negative - hidden risk","False positive - wasted remediation effort and alert fatigue","True positive - patch immediately","True negative - expected result"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Reporting a flaw that does not exist is a false positive - each one burns analyst time and erodes trust in the scanner. The dangerous inverse, a FALSE NEGATIVE, is a real flaw the scanner missed: invisible risk.$q$),
('q4-012',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$Two vulnerabilities: (A) CVSS 9.1 on an internal test box with no sensitive data; (B) CVSS 7.8, known-exploited, on an internet-facing payment server. Which do you remediate FIRST and why?$q$,$q$["A - higher CVSS always wins","B - exposure, active exploitation, and asset criticality outweigh raw score","Neither - wait for vendor guidance","Both simultaneously - priority is impossible"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Prioritization combines severity WITH exploitability (KEV/active exploitation), exposure (internet-facing), and business impact (payment data). B is being exploited in the wild against your most exposed, most valuable asset. CVSS is one input, not the decision.$q$),
('q4-013',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A vulnerability cannot be patched for 90 days due to vendor dependencies. The risk owner signs a document accepting the risk with added monitoring until then. What is this called?$q$,$q$["False positive handling","An exception/exemption with compensating controls","Insurance transfer","Decommissioning"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A documented, time-bound exception records formal risk acceptance while compensating controls (monitoring, segmentation) reduce exposure until the patch lands. Insurance transfers financial impact; decommissioning retires systems.$q$),
('q4-014',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$After patching a critical vulnerability, what MUST happen before the finding is closed?$q$,$q$["Update the risk appetite statement","Rescan/verify to confirm remediation actually worked","Notify the vendor","Rotate all passwords"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Validation of remediation - rescanning or retesting - confirms the fix applied and the exposure is gone; patches fail silently more often than teams expect. Then reporting closes the loop. The other actions are situational, not the required closure step.$q$),
('q4-015',4,'4.3','mcq',3,$q$What is the key difference between how a bug bounty program and a scheduled penetration test discover vulnerabilities?$q$,$q$["Bounties are illegal; pen tests are legal","Bounties crowdsource continuous testing from many researchers paid per finding; pen tests are scoped, time-boxed engagements by a contracted team","Pen tests never produce reports","Bounties only cover physical security"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Bug bounties run continuously with diverse independent researchers rewarded per validated finding under disclosure rules; pen tests deliver a systematic, scoped assessment with a report at a point in time. Both are legal, authorized approaches that complement each other.$q$),
('q4-016',4,'4.4','mcq',1,$q$Which system aggregates logs from firewalls, servers, and endpoints, correlates events across them, and raises alerts?$q$,$q$["DLP","SIEM","NAC","WAF"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$That is the SIEM's definition: aggregation, normalization, correlation, alerting, retention. DLP watches data leaving; NAC gates network admission; WAF filters web traffic.$q$),
('q4-017',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$Failed VPN logins from one source, followed minutes later by a successful login and an unusual admin group change, generate a single high-priority alert. Which SIEM capability produced this?$q$,$q$["Log archiving","Event correlation","Alert tuning","Packet capture"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Linking related events across sources and time into one meaningful detection is correlation - the SIEM's core value beyond storage. Archiving retains; tuning adjusts thresholds; packet capture records traffic.$q$),
('q4-018',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$The SOC receives 5,000 alerts daily; analysts admit they ignore most. What is the FIRST corrective step?$q$,$q$["Hire 50 more analysts","Tune alert rules/thresholds to cut false positives and prioritize by severity","Disable the SIEM","Route all alerts to email"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Alert fatigue means real incidents drown in noise; tuning (adjusting rules, thresholds, suppression, prioritization) restores signal. Scaling headcount against noise is wasteful; disabling monitoring or shifting the channel fixes nothing.$q$),
('q4-019',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$Which tool verifies that critical system files have not been altered by comparing current hashes to a known-good baseline?$q$,$q$["DLP","File integrity monitoring","NetFlow","Web filter"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$FIM detects tampering with configs, binaries, and system files via hash comparison - required by PCI DSS and vital for catching persistence mechanisms. DLP tracks data egress; NetFlow summarizes traffic; web filters control browsing.$q$),
('q4-020',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$An analyst needs to know WHICH internal hosts communicated with a known-malicious IP over the last month, without payload content. Which data source answers fastest?$q$,$q$["Full packet captures","NetFlow records","Application debug logs","Vulnerability scans"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$NetFlow is exactly this: compact who-talked-to-whom metadata retained affordably at scale. Month-long full captures are usually impractical to store/search; app logs cover single systems; scans list flaws, not conversations.$q$),
('q4-021',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$Which protocol version should be REQUIRED for managing network devices, and why?$q$,$q$["SNMPv1 - simplest","SNMPv2c - community strings","SNMPv3 - authentication and encryption","Telnet - universal support"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Only SNMPv3 authenticates users and encrypts management traffic; v1/v2c send community strings (passwords) in cleartext. Telnet is a cleartext remote shell - replaced by SSH.$q$),
('q4-022',4,'4.4','mcq',3,$q$How does SCAP help maintain secure configurations at scale?$q$,$q$["It replaces the SIEM","It automates checking systems against benchmarks using standardized content (OVAL/XCCDF), making compliance scanning vendor-neutral","It encrypts configuration files","It blocks configuration changes"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SCAP standardizes how configuration/vulnerability checks are expressed and evaluated, so tools from any vendor can assess systems against the same benchmark content automatically - continuous baseline compliance at scale.$q$),
('q4-023',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$What principle should the FINAL rule in a firewall policy implement?$q$,$q$["Allow all remaining traffic","Implicit/explicit deny of everything not previously permitted","Log-only mode","Rate limiting"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Least privilege for networks: permit specific required traffic, deny everything else by default via the final deny rule. Ending with allow-all inverts the model and negates the policy.$q$),
('q4-024',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$An IPS blocks a legitimate business application after a signature update. What operational practice reduces this risk for future updates?$q$,$q$["Disable the IPS permanently","Test signatures in monitor/alert mode or a staging segment before enforcing inline","Switch to IDS-only forever","Whitelist all internal traffic"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$New signatures should run in detect-only or staging first, validating against real traffic before blocking - balancing protection with availability. Permanent disablement or blanket whitelisting abandons the control.$q$),
('q4-025',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$Which email authentication mechanism lets a domain owner PUBLISH POLICY for how receivers should treat messages failing SPF/DKIM, plus receive reports?$q$,$q$["S/MIME","DMARC","POP3S","Greylisting"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DMARC builds on SPF (authorized senders) and DKIM (signatures): the domain publishes reject/quarantine/none policy and receives aggregate reports on abuse. S/MIME encrypts/signs message content; POP3S secures mailbox retrieval; greylisting delays unknown senders.$q$),
('q4-026',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$A user's laptop fails the NAC health check (missing EDR agent). What SHOULD happen next in a well-designed deployment?$q$,$q$["Permanent network ban","Placement in a remediation VLAN with access limited to update/patch resources","Full network access with a warning email","Device wipe"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Posture-failing devices go to a quarantine/remediation segment where they can fetch the missing agent/patches, then re-check and join. Banning or wiping is disproportionate; admitting noncompliant devices defeats NAC's purpose.$q$),
('q4-027',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$What advantage does agent-based web filtering have over an on-premises proxy appliance for a remote workforce?$q$,$q$["It is always free","Policy follows the device anywhere - no need to backhaul traffic to the office","It inspects encrypted traffic without any certificate","It blocks physical attacks"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The agent enforces filtering on the endpoint itself wherever it roams; a central proxy protects only traffic routed through it (VPN backhaul adds latency). TLS inspection still needs trust configuration either way.$q$),
('q4-028',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$Which control stops users from resolving and reaching newly registered phishing domains at the NETWORK level, before a web session even starts?$q$,$q$["EDR","DNS filtering","FIM","Port security"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DNS filtering blocks resolution of malicious/newly-registered domains - the connection dies before HTTP begins; cheap and broad. EDR acts on endpoint behavior; FIM watches files; port security controls switch ports.$q$),
('q4-029',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$What distinguishes EDR from traditional antivirus?$q$,$q$["EDR is signature-only","EDR continuously records endpoint behavior, enabling detection, investigation (timeline), and response actions like host isolation","Antivirus cannot run on servers","EDR requires no updates"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$EDR adds telemetry recording, behavioral analytics, hunting, and response (isolate host, kill process, roll back) - AV's signature/heuristic blocking is one layer within it. The other statements are false.$q$),
('q4-030',4,'4.5','mcq',3,$q$A security team wants detections correlated automatically across endpoints, email, identity, and cloud workloads in one investigation view. Which technology category is this?$q$,$q$["HIDS","XDR","SWG","UPS"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Extended Detection and Response (XDR) unifies telemetry and detection across multiple layers beyond the endpoint - correlating an email lure, endpoint execution, and suspicious cloud login into one incident. HIDS is host-only; SWG filters web; UPS is power.$q$),
('q4-031',4,'4.5','mcq',2,$q$Which Group Policy-enforced settings directly support account security? (Choose the BEST set.)$q$,$q$["Desktop wallpaper and screensaver art","Password length/complexity, account lockout threshold, session lock timeout","Default browser homepage","Printer mappings"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$GPOs centrally enforce authentication-related policy: password requirements, lockout after failed attempts, and automatic session locks. Cosmetic and convenience settings are manageable but not security controls.$q$),
('q4-032',4,'4.6','mcq',1,$q$A new hire starts Monday; access is created through a formal process granting exactly the systems her role requires. Which TWO IAM concepts are shown?$q$,$q$["Provisioning and least privilege","Federation and SSO","Attestation and PAM","Deprovisioning and MFA"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Creating accounts/rights through governed onboarding is provisioning; scoping them to the role's needs is least privilege. Federation/SSO concern login mechanics; attestation reviews existing access; deprovisioning is the exit process.$q$),
('q4-033',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$Audit finds active accounts belonging to employees who left months ago. Which process failed, and what is the direct risk?$q$,$q$["Provisioning; too little access","Deprovisioning; orphaned accounts usable by ex-employees or attackers","Federation; broken trust","Password policy; weak complexity"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Offboarding/deprovisioning must disable access promptly at separation - orphaned accounts are unmonitored, valid credentials for ex-insiders and credential thieves. The other options do not describe stale active accounts.$q$),
('q4-034',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$In a SAML federation, which component authenticates the user and issues the signed assertion?$q$,$q$["Service provider","Identity provider","Certificate authority","RADIUS client"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The IdP authenticates (password + MFA) and issues signed assertions; the SP consumes and trusts them, never seeing the password. CAs issue certificates; RADIUS clients are network devices in AAA, not SAML parties.$q$),
('q4-035',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$Which combination constitutes TRUE multifactor authentication?$q$,$q$["Password + PIN","Password + TOTP app code","PIN + security questions","Two different passwords"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Password (something you know) + TOTP from a registered device (something you have) crosses two factor CATEGORIES. Passwords, PINs, and security questions are all knowledge - stacking them is single-factor, multiple times.$q$);
